---
// Search. The one page here that does not work without JavaScript, and it
// says so rather than leaving a reader who has it off with a box that does
// nothing.
//
// The index is a file in the build like everything else, so the search runs
// in the browser against a file it fetched. No query anybody types on this
// site is sent anywhere.
import Shell from "../layouts/Shell.astro";
import Search from "../components/Search.tsx";
import { LANG_NAMES, href, searchLangs, titles } from "../site";

const langs = searchLangs();
// Built here rather than inline in the sentence, because two expressions
// side by side in the markup run together with nothing between them.
const count = `${langs.length} ${langs.length === 1 ? "language" : "languages"}`;
---

<Shell title="Search" here="search">
  <h1>Search</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Every word of every paper here, in {count}. The index is a file this page
    downloads the first time you type in the box, and the search runs in your
    browser. Nothing you type leaves it.
  </p>

  <noscript>
    <p class="missing">
      This page needs JavaScript, because the searching happens in your browser
      rather than on a server. Everything else here is plain pages and works
      without it: the <a href={href("")}>catalogue</a> lists every paper by field
      and by year.
    </p>
  </noscript>

  <Search
    client:load
    base={import.meta.env.BASE_URL}
    langs={langs}
    names={LANG_NAMES}
    titles={titles()}
  />

  <h2>What it looks in</h2>
  <p>
    Every block of every page: the paragraphs, the headings, the formulas and
    the program listings. The prose and the mathematics are indexed apart, so a
    word you typed finds the paragraph that uses it before the formula that
    happens to contain the same letters.
  </p>
  <p>
    A word in more than a third of the blocks of a language is left out of that
    language's index. It could not narrow a search down and it was most of the
    file. That is why searching for the returns nothing, in any of the four.
  </p>
  <p>
    Chinese and Japanese are cut into overlapping pairs of characters rather
    than into words. It is crude, it finds short words inside longer ones, and
    it is what a corpus this size can afford without shipping a segmenter to
    every reader.
  </p>
  <p>
    Results are per block and not per paper, so a long paper that mentions your
    word in two places is two lines and each one goes to the section it is in.
    No paper gets more than three lines, or one paper that keeps saying the
    word would be the whole page. The last word you type also matches on its
    beginning, which is why the list keeps moving while you are still typing
    it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The two lines under a result are the section it is in and the opening of
    the block. The opening, and not the sentence that matched: the index
    carries the first part of every block so that a page of results is not a
    page of downloads, and in a long paragraph your word may be further in
    than the part you can see.
  </p>
</Shell>
